1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:03,700
What does a lunar laser normal point actually record?

2
00:00:03,650 --> 00:00:08,962
ILRS normal points collect full-rate observations, not a single pulse.

3
00:00:08,962 --> 00:00:14,725
A station times returning light many times, yet an archive may report one summary.

4
00:00:14,725 --> 00:00:16,762
Why is that useful?

5
00:00:16,762 --> 00:00:22,300
Our ledger keeps four columns: measured, dated, counted, assumed.

6
00:00:22,300 --> 00:00:25,587
One reported row cannot replace them all.

7
00:00:25,587 --> 00:00:32,200
Conditional calculation: a summary depends on accepted observations and stated processing rules.

8
00:00:32,200 --> 00:00:34,737
We will not invent measurements.

9
00:00:34,737 --> 00:00:38,225
Station one asks what arrives at the detector.

10
00:00:38,225 --> 00:00:43,975
A ranging station transmits a laser pulse toward a reflector and seeks a returning signal.

11
00:00:43,975 --> 00:00:52,750
Recorded arrival timing belongs to a physical experiment; that one return cannot, by itself, establish a secular orbital trend.

12
00:00:52,750 --> 00:00:59,725
ILRS distinguishes full-rate measurements and normal points, which summarize observations over an interval.

13
00:00:59,725 --> 00:01:03,450
Its normal-point bin for lunar targets is variable.

14
00:01:03,450 --> 00:01:08,400
This is an ILRS product definition, not an actual dated measurement.

15
00:01:08,400 --> 00:01:15,425
No specific count, epoch, or uncertainty is assigned to this pictured Apollo fourteen laser reflector.

16
00:01:15,425 --> 00:01:19,000
Every observation also belongs to an epoch.

17
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:24,487
As the Earth rotates and the Moon travels along its orbit, geometry changes.

18
00:01:24,487 --> 00:01:30,962
Dates therefore cannot be removed just because several observations are collected into a more compact product.

19
00:01:30,962 --> 00:01:34,625
Their provenance makes meaningful comparisons possible.

20
00:01:34,625 --> 00:01:40,200
Imagine four hypothetical pulses shown as pencil marks on a blank notebook page.

21
00:01:40,200 --> 00:01:44,912
Four is a deliberately assumed illustration, not a sample from NASA.

22
00:01:44,912 --> 00:01:51,075
We assign no invented nanoseconds, station label, or lunar target to those marks.

23
00:01:51,075 --> 00:01:55,500
They show repeated observations, not a historical dataset.

24
00:01:55,500 --> 00:02:01,775
Some observations might fail a documented quality test; others might need a stated correction.

25
00:02:01,775 --> 00:02:05,962
An honest diagram does not guess which ones were rejected.

26
00:02:05,962 --> 00:02:13,025
It shows a separate selection step marked conditional and sends the viewer to the data specifications for the real rules.

27
00:02:13,025 --> 00:02:18,850
The ILRS data pages document multiple formats and classes of available records.

28
00:02:18,850 --> 00:02:25,012
A format explains how information is represented, not whether every supplied record is perfect.

29
00:02:25,012 --> 00:02:33,725
Proper provenance links the station, reflector target, observation time and processing information, rather than displaying an orphan number.

30
00:02:33,725 --> 00:02:47,062
At the first station's end, our ledger reads measured: returned-light timing; dated: observation epochs; counted: accepted records, when documented; assumed: reduction method and model.

31
00:02:47,062 --> 00:02:53,887
None of these four columns should silently substitute for another merely because a video has limited screen space.

32
00:02:53,887 --> 00:02:58,125
Station two asks what a summary keeps and what it leaves out.

33
00:02:58,125 --> 00:03:05,775
A normal point is a processed record useful for subsequent analysis, not another physical photon arriving at the detector.

34
00:03:05,775 --> 00:03:11,275
Calling it a single raw pulse would turn a database product into the wrong kind of evidence.

35
00:03:11,275 --> 00:03:17,275
Our teaching page draws four empty event marks leading to a single outlined summary box.

36
00:03:17,275 --> 00:03:22,387
That is an illustrative workflow only, not an official normal-point algorithm.

37
00:03:22,387 --> 00:03:30,400
The diagram displays the conditional statement: if the selection and reduction rules are satisfied, a summary may be constructed.

38
00:03:30,400 --> 00:03:34,812
Why is ordinary arithmetic averaging not necessarily enough?

39
00:03:34,812 --> 00:03:39,237
The underlying observations can have different quality or corrections.

40
00:03:39,237 --> 00:03:44,237
A simple average of made-up values would not certify an ILRS record.

41
00:03:44,237 --> 00:03:52,225
For the actual procedure, the reduction convention and associated uncertainty definitions have to be checked in primary documentation.

42
00:03:52,225 --> 00:03:55,187
One complication is the atmosphere.

43
00:03:55,187 --> 00:04:04,825
A simple classroom model uses two-way light travel and the speed of light, but professional range reductions can include atmospheric effects and station calibration.

44
00:04:04,825 --> 00:04:11,975
These corrections must be identified from documentation rather than assigned imaginary numerical sizes in the animation.

45
00:04:11,975 --> 00:04:15,412
Suppose two records came from different epochs.

46
00:04:15,412 --> 00:04:19,987
Their values would not directly give a rate at which the Moon is receding.

47
00:04:19,987 --> 00:04:25,350
Ground-station position, lunar reflector geometry and orbital state change.

48
00:04:25,350 --> 00:04:32,375
Fitting a long-term orbital parameter requires a model relating many dated observations to the evolving geometry.

49
00:04:32,524 --> 00:04:40,999
This is why the previous episode's published thirty eight point three zero millimetres per year is not a value printed inside every normal point.

50
00:04:40,949 --> 00:04:45,211
Williams and Boggs present it as a fitted lunar-orbit parameter.

51
00:04:45,211 --> 00:04:49,436
We keep that rate in a different evidence box from pulse timings.

52
00:04:49,436 --> 00:04:57,311
The published uncertainty on a fitted rate likewise does not automatically describe the error on one pulse or one normal-point product.

53
00:04:57,311 --> 00:05:01,324
Different statistics can belong to the same research programme.

54
00:05:01,324 --> 00:05:07,161
Putting their units and definitions beside the numbers prevents an attractive but false comparison.

55
00:05:07,161 --> 00:05:10,124
Now we can close the original question.

56
00:05:10,124 --> 00:05:18,786
A reduced data product helps observations enter a scientific workflow, but it is not proof that one pulse measures the changing Earth-Moon system.

57
00:05:18,786 --> 00:05:25,336
We need the original timing, epoch, reduction assumptions and dynamical fit to follow the inference.

58
00:05:25,336 --> 00:05:30,361
Station three puts two other sources outside our illustrative reduction.

59
00:05:30,361 --> 00:05:34,686
NASA explains the retroreflector and laser's round trip.

60
00:05:34,686 --> 00:05:38,561
A peer-reviewed orbital analysis reports a fitted rate.

61
00:05:38,561 --> 00:05:45,361
Neither source can replace the original processing documentation for a specific ILRS normal-point record.

62
00:05:45,361 --> 00:05:52,049
A photograph of equipment might show the reflector arrangement, but not the weighting applied to observational data.

63
00:05:52,049 --> 00:06:01,511
Conversely, a journal abstract can report a statistically estimated orbit rate without listing which individual return events entered a particular processed record.

64
00:06:01,511 --> 00:06:05,211
We separate these evidentiary roles explicitly.

65
00:06:05,211 --> 00:06:13,136
A scientific archive is valuable because researchers can seek documented data products and compare the conventions used to create them.

66
00:06:13,136 --> 00:06:17,674
This is stronger than pretending that every summary is beyond criticism.

67
00:06:17,674 --> 00:06:24,099
Uncertainty assessment and data selection still deserve inspection when the scientific question changes.

68
00:06:24,099 --> 00:06:30,024
The word normal is a technical label, not a promise that the value is ordinary or error-free.

69
00:06:30,024 --> 00:06:36,474
Viewers might otherwise imagine one perfect return photon represented by one perfect distance.

70
00:06:36,474 --> 00:06:41,936
On-screen labels instead identify the record as a processed observational product.

71
00:06:41,936 --> 00:06:46,149
A compelling graph with invented points would remain an invention.

72
00:06:46,149 --> 00:06:52,424
Our graphic deliberately uses empty data cells where no actual observation file has been opened.

73
00:06:52,424 --> 00:07:01,299
A future numerical worked example must identify an archive record and its fields, not borrow a persuasive shape from an unrelated illustration.

74
00:07:01,299 --> 00:07:09,411
Ancient coral counting and modern laser observations share a methodological question: what exactly is counted, and by which rule?

75
00:07:09,411 --> 00:07:19,174
The objects and scientific uncertainties are entirely different, so we cannot turn a coral band into a laser return or treat their numerical counts as interchangeable.

76
00:07:19,174 --> 00:07:23,849
At the third station's end, the unresolved item is written openly.

77
00:07:23,849 --> 00:07:30,736
We have not verified a specific normal-point record's event count, time span or statistical uncertainty.

78
00:07:30,736 --> 00:07:37,811
We can explain the data categories and the route to a reproducible example without manufacturing those missing fields.

79
00:07:37,811 --> 00:07:49,649
At the ledger table, measured means timed observations; dated means their epochs; counted means accepted items if actually documented; assumed means reduction and dynamical conventions.

80
00:07:49,649 --> 00:07:56,099
We retain those four headings throughout so that the final result can be traced backward instead of simply admired.

81
00:07:56,099 --> 00:08:02,524
Entry one records ILRS documentation as the source for full-rate and normal-point categories.

82
00:08:02,524 --> 00:08:05,824
No station-specific number is attached.

83
00:08:05,824 --> 00:08:13,786
The source is a public archive description, and its value here is to define the types of scientific records used for laser ranging.

84
00:08:13,786 --> 00:08:19,436
Entry two records NASA's explanation of retroreflection and round-trip light timing.

85
00:08:19,436 --> 00:08:24,324
It supports the physical principle, not a fictional normal-point variance.

86
00:08:24,324 --> 00:08:33,174
A named instrument photograph would require a separate image citation; we do not claim the generic drawing is an accurately scaled Apollo artefact.

87
00:08:33,340 --> 00:08:40,265
Entry three records a separate model-derived result from Williams and Boggs, expressed in millimetres per year.

88
00:08:40,215 --> 00:08:44,490
The units alone show that this result is not a pulse travel time.

89
00:08:44,490 --> 00:08:50,340
A source marker and DOI remain visible beside the research claim on the notebook page.

90
00:08:50,340 --> 00:09:01,865
Entry four lists work that a deeper archival replication would require: the actual observation file, reduction specification, number of input events, and uncertainty convention.

91
00:09:01,865 --> 00:09:05,015
Its blank numerical cells are deliberate.

92
00:09:05,015 --> 00:09:08,840
A blank with a method is preferable to invented precision.

93
00:09:08,840 --> 00:09:19,890
Three choices can change a numerical demonstration: selecting a different documented time interval, applying a different documented correction, or analysing another published archive release.

94
00:09:19,890 --> 00:09:26,027
Such changes do not mean the Moon itself suddenly changed speed when the notebook page was turned.

95
00:09:26,027 --> 00:09:29,252
Return to the Apollo fourteen reflector.

96
00:09:29,252 --> 00:09:34,340
Its photograph is a real object, but it is not a single normal-point record.

97
00:09:34,340 --> 00:09:40,615
ILRS normal points collect full-rate observations into intervals; lunar bins are variable.

98
00:09:40,615 --> 00:09:46,452
An image alone cannot establish an epoch, measured return count, or uncertainty.

99
00:09:46,452 --> 00:09:51,952
Next in Strata Notes: how lunar orbit models shape a fitted recession estimate.

